  "text": "Assistant: To solve the problem, we need to find a point \\( E \\) inside the parallelogram \\( ABCD \\) such that the four small triangles \\( AEB \\), \\( AEC \\), \\( BED \\), and \\( CED \\) all have equal perimeters. We'll first calculate the perimeter of the parallelogram and then determine the coordinates of point \\( E \\) that ensures all four triangles have equal perimeters.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the perimeter of the parallelogram \\( ABCD \\)\nThe perimeter of a parallelogram is the sum of the lengths of its four sides. The lengths of the sides can be calculated using the distance formula:\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2} \\]\n\nCalculate the lengths of the sides:\n\\[ AB = \\sqrt{(6-2)^2 + (5-3)^2} = \\sqrt{16 + 4} = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\\[ BC = \\sqrt{(8-6)^2 + (9-5)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 16} = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\\[ CD = \\sqrt{(4-8)^2 + (7-9)^2} = \\sqrt{16 + 4} = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\\[ DA = \\sqrt{(2-4)^2 + (3-7)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 16} = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\nThe perimeter of the parallelogram is:\n\\[ \\text{Perimeter} = 2 \\times (AB + BC) = 2 \\times (2\\sqrt{5} + 2\\sqrt{5}) = 8\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the perimeter of each small triangle\nEach small triangle has a perimeter equal to one-fourth of the perimeter of the parallelogram:\n\\[ \\text{Perimeter of each small triangle} = \\frac{8\\sqrt{5}}{4} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the coordinates of point \\( E \\)\nThe perimeter of each small triangle is the sum of the lengths of the sides of the triangles. Let \\( E = (x, y) \\). The distances from \\( E \\) to the vertices of the triangles will help us find the coordinates of \\( E \\).\n\nThe distances from \\( E \\) to the vertices of the triangles are:\n\\[ AE = \\sqrt{(x-2)^2 + (y-3)^2} \\]\n\\[ BE = \\sqrt{(x-6)^2 + (y-5)^2} \\]\n\\[ CE = \\sqrt{(x-8)^2 + (y-9)^2} \\]\n\\[ DE = \\sqrt{(x-4)^2 + (y-7)^2} \\]\n\nFor the perimeters to be equal:\n\\[ AE + BE = \\sqrt{(x-2)^2 + (y-3)^2} + \\sqrt{(x-6)^2 + (y-5)^2} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\\[ AE + CE = \\sqrt{(x-2)^2 + (y-3)^2} + \\sqrt{(x-8)^2 + (y-9)^2} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\\[ AE + DE = \\sqrt{(x-2)^2 + (y-3)^2} + \\sqrt{(x-4)^2 + (y-7)^2} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\\[ BE + CE = \\sqrt{(x-6)^2 + (y-5)^2} + \\sqrt{(x-8)^2 + (y-9)^2} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\\[ BE + DE = \\sqrt{(x-6)^2 + (y-5)^2} + \\sqrt{(x-4)^2 + (y-7)^2} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\\[ CE + DE = \\sqrt{(x-8)^2 + (y-9)^2} + \\sqrt{(x-4)^2 + (y-7)^2} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\nBy symmetry and solving these equations, we find that the coordinates \\( E \\) are:\n\\[ E = (6, 6) \\]\n\n### Step 4: Calculate \\( 5x + 2y \\)\nSubstitute \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( y = 6 \\) into \\( 5x + 2y \\):\n\\[ 5x + 2y = 5 \\times 6 + 2 \\times 6 = 30 + 12 = 42 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( 5x + 2y \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{42} \\]",
